Step 1 — Create Project
Open Visual Studio 2019.
1. Create a new project
2. Select ASP.NET Web Application (.NET Framework)
3. Project name: StudentRegistration
4. Select .NET Framework 4.7.2
5. Select MVC
6. Authentication: No Authentication
7. Click Create.     Practical10
Step 2 — Create Student Model
Right-click:
Models → Add → Class
Name:
Student.cs
Add:
using System.ComponentModel.DataAnnotations;

namespace StudentRegistration.Models
{
    public class Student
    {
        [Required]
        public string Name { get; set; }

        [Required]
        public string Email { get; set; }

        [Required]
        public string Password { get; set; }

        [Required]
        public string Gender { get; set; }

        [Required]
        public string Course { get; set; }

        public bool Agree { get; set; }
    }
}

The [Required] attributes are used for validation.     Practical10
Step 3 — Create Student Controller
Right-click:
Controllers → Add → Controller
Select:
MVC 5 Controller - Empty
Name:
StudentController
Replace the code with:
using System.Web.Mvc;
using StudentRegistration.Models;

namespace StudentRegistration.Controllers
{
    public class StudentController : Controller
    {
        public ActionResult Register()
        {
            return View();
        }

        [HttpPost]
        public ActionResult Register(Student student)
        {
            if (ModelState.IsValid)
            {
                ViewBag.Message = "Student registered successfully!";
            }

            return View(student);
        }
    }
}

The first Register() displays the form, while the [HttpPost] method receives the submitted data.     Practical10
Step 4 — Create Register View
Right-click inside:
public ActionResult Register()

Select Add View.
Set:
- View name: Register
- Template: Empty
- Model class: Student
Click Add.
It creates:
Views/Student/Register.cshtml     Practical10
Step 5 — Add Registration Form
Open:
Views/Student/Register.cshtml
Add:
@model StudentRegistration.Models.Student

@{
    ViewBag.Title = "Student Registration";
}

<h2>Student Registration</h2>

@using (Html.BeginForm())
{
    <div>
        @Html.LabelFor(m => m.Name)
        @Html.TextBoxFor(m => m.Name)
        @Html.ValidationMessageFor(m => m.Name)
    </div>

    <br />

    <div>
        @Html.LabelFor(m => m.Email)
        @Html.TextBoxFor(m => m.Email)
        @Html.ValidationMessageFor(m => m.Email)
    </div>

    <br />

    <div>
        @Html.LabelFor(m => m.Password)
        @Html.PasswordFor(m => m.Password)
        @Html.ValidationMessageFor(m => m.Password)
    </div>

    <br />

    <div>
        @Html.LabelFor(m => m.Gender)
        @Html.RadioButtonFor(m => m.Gender, "Male")
        Male

        @Html.RadioButtonFor(m => m.Gender, "Female")
        Female
    </div>

    <br />

    <div>
        @Html.LabelFor(m => m.Course)

        @Html.DropDownListFor(
            m => m.Course,
            new SelectList(new[]
            {
                "BSc Data Science",
                "BSc IT",
                "BCA",
                "MCA"
            }),
            "Select Course"
        )
    </div>

    <br />

    <div>
        @Html.CheckBoxFor(m => m.Agree)
        I agree to the terms and conditions.
    </div>

    <br />

    <input type="submit" value="Register" />
}

@if (ViewBag.Message != null)
{
    <h3>@ViewBag.Message</h3>
}

